## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to design a scalable, governed, multi-region data lake with reliable ingestion, deduplication, and schema evolution.

## Key Concepts

- Regional ingestion paths and Parquet data lake
- Lake Formation governance and CRR
- Idempotent ingestion with event_id dedup
- Schema evolution via Avro + Glue Schema Registry
- Late data handling and e2e testing

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudocode dedupe
async function isDuplicate(event_id){
  const exists = await dynamo.get({PK: event_id});
  if (exists) return true;
  await dynamo.put({PK: event_id, ...});
  return false;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you scale CRR for thousands of regions?
- How would you monitor data quality drift in this setup?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests handling of incremental, tombstone-aware ingestion, schema drift, cross-account governance, and robust testing in a scalable AWS data lake.

## Key Concepts

- Tombstones for deletes and upserts in a lake
- Hourly partitioning and offset tracking
- Schema evolution with Glue and registry
- Lake Formation cross-account access and fine‑grained permissions
- End-to-end testing including backfills and rollback plans

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-code: read offset, decrypt, write Parquet hourly, handle tombstones
const last = db.get("offset");
const files = listOrcFilesSince(last);
for (const f of files) {
  const data = decrypt(read(f));
  const upserted = transformToParquet(data);
  s3.putParquet(upserted, `hourly/${f.hour}`);
  if (data.deleted) tombstonePut(f.id);
  db.set("offset", f.hour);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you detect and handle schema drift across multiple source versions?
- How would you test rollback and backfill scenarios across accounts?
